  "query_preview": "..\n\nProgressive shortwave ridging aloft will push across the Great Lakes \ntoday supporting surface high pressure. Differential geopotential \nheight rises and increased thickness will support a notable uptick \nin daytime temperatures. Highs today are expected to be near 80 \ndegrees which is right at lake August seasonal averages.\n\nNext Pacific upper level jet packet and reasonably well organized \nupper level low pressure circulation will dig into Lower Michigan \nWednesday and Wednesday night. Residual stability and subsaturated \nairmass between 3.5 and 9.0 kft agl will lead to quiet weather \nconditions for early and midday Wednesday. Will likely be looking at \ntwo thetae warm front structures the first bringing iso-sct showers \nand virga 16-20z, the second associated with cyclonic flow and \ngreater convergence along a prefrontal trough at 23z Wed to 05z Thu. \nWind progs suggest 850mb winds/LLJ increasing +30 knots immediately \nin advance of the prefrontal trough contributing to 0-6km bulk shear \nmagnitudes of 30-35 knots. Forecast soundings continue to show \nstatically stable conditions throughout the lowest 4.5 kft agl which \ncasts doubt on whether or not thunderstorm activity will be \nelevated. Regardless, marginal instability at around or less than \n1000 J/kg limits much in the way of widespread severe weather. \nLatest Swody2 has the majority of Southeast Michigan in a Marginal \nrisk for severe weather primarily for the late afternoon and early \nevening hours. A portion of western Lenawee County is designated in \na Slight risk for severe weather as there is some potential for \nsupercells to the southwest of the area.\n\nModels suggest a good amount of wave dampening which results in \nnothing more than a spoke of absolute vorticity Thursday. Deep \ncyclonic flow with steeper lapse rates aloft brings an isolated \nchance of showers Thursday afternoon.  Good confidence in additional \nhumidity on Thursday with dewpoints then dropping behind a cold \nfront Friday.\n\nConfidence appears to be increasing that any Pacific shortwave \nenergy will wait until very late in the weekend. GFS has come more \nin line with the ECMWF in keeping surface high pressure in place \nSaturday.\n\nLower confidence exists regarding the forecast next weekend. There \nis some global deterministic differences on when the next Pacific \njet packet will push into the Great Lakes region. Another very nice \nlate summer weekend looks likely.",
